Lab 9: Implementing Camel Transactions
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Configure and manage transactions in Camel routes to ensure data consistency.
Implement distributed transactions using Camel.
Use XA transactions for managing multiple resources.
Set up transaction rollback and commit strategies.
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, ensure you have:

Basic knowledge of Apache Camel and Java.
JDK 11 or later installed.
Maven installed (for dependency management).
A running instance of a transactional resource (e.g., PostgreSQL, ActiveMQ).
An IDE (e.g., IntelliJ, VS Code, or Eclipse).
Lab Setup
Step 1: Create a Maven Project
Open a terminal and run the following command to generate a new Maven project:
mvn archetype:generate -DgroupId=com.example -DartifactId=camel-transactions -DarchetypeArtifactId=maven-archetype-quickstart -DinteractiveMode=false
Navigate to the project directory:
cd camel-transactions
Step 2: Add Dependencies
Update the pom.xml file with the following dependencies:

<dependencies>
    <!-- Camel Core -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-core</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    
    <!-- Camel Spring Boot (for transaction management) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel.springboot</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    
    <!-- Transaction Manager (JTA) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-jta-atomikos</artifactId>
        <version>2.7.0</version>
    </dependency>
    
    <!-- Database (PostgreSQL) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
        <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
